\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Tokenization bias on a 1st-order Markov chain. Given the context token ``A'', the model never samples the next token ``A'' (red, probability $0.0$), rather than with probability $1-\alpha $. In practice, this bias occurs when prompts end with tokens that are part of another token, a common issue in FIM tasks, leading to incorrect completions by the model.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:markov1}{{2}{4}{Tokenization bias on a 1st-order Markov chain. Given the context token ``A'', the model never samples the next token ``A'' (red, probability $0.0$), rather than with probability $1-\alpha $. In practice, this bias occurs when prompts end with tokens that are part of another token, a common issue in FIM tasks, leading to incorrect completions by the model}{figure.2}{}}
