\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Pass rates (\%) on the HumanEval Random Span infilling benchmark with CodeLlama-7B. @10 is the maximum over a temperature sweep. Healing and alignment are only applied in SPM mode.}}{8}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:fim}{{1}{8}{Pass rates (\%) on the HumanEval Random Span infilling benchmark with CodeLlama-7B. @10 is the maximum over a temperature sweep. Healing and alignment are only applied in SPM mode}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces pass@10 on the HumanEval Random Span benchmark \citep  {bavarian2022fim} with CodeLlama-7B as a function of sampling temperature, for PSM, SPM and SPM with token healing \citep  {dagan2024getting}. With byte-level predictions (left), the three curves essentially coincide; with token-level predictions (right), SPM is far below PSM at all temperatures shown, and healing recovers most of the gap. The SPM template forces the model to predict from the middle of a token, which byte-level predictions handle.}}{8}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fim}{{6}{8}{pass@10 on the HumanEval Random Span benchmark \citep {bavarian2022fim} with CodeLlama-7B as a function of sampling temperature, for PSM, SPM and SPM with token healing \citep {dagan2024getting}. With byte-level predictions (left), the three curves essentially coincide; with token-level predictions (right), SPM is far below PSM at all temperatures shown, and healing recovers most of the gap. The SPM template forces the model to predict from the middle of a token, which byte-level predictions handle}{figure.6}{}}
